u have a table: A B 3 A B 10\n\nThe first query would eliminate both rows, the second only one. Alternatively,\nif you have NULL values in status_code column, you may get different results,\n(A or not A) does not work in SQL if you have NULL values in columns.\n\n"""'}], 'response': 'Case 1:\nThe root cause of the long runtime is that the planner has rewritten the query embedding the view code in an inefficient way. The proposed solution uses a CTE (Common Table Expression) to force the planner to materialize the selection from the views, which improves the performance.\n\nCase 2:\nThe issue with the update query is that it tries to select from the same table being updated, which is not allowed in MySQL. The proposed solution uses UPDATE IGNORE to avoid duplicate conflicts and INSERT ON DUPLICATE KEY to frame an INSERT query that always creates a duplicate conflict and then updates the part.\n\nCase 3:\nThe two queries are different because of the way MINUS works. MINUS eliminates all rows from the first query that have matches in the second query, whereas NOT IN only eliminates rows where the status_code is exactly one of the values (3 or 600). If there are duplicate records with different status_codes, the MINUS query will eliminate them, but the NOT IN query will not.', 'time': 1.9503750801086426}
